%We give a detailed proofs in the following subsections, but first we make a comment. While our extended results for the $q$-Fractional problem are strictly stronger for the class of polynomial systems restricted by any particular degree bound of at least 2 (and even for the additionally-restricted class where every polynomial contains at most 3 variables, as in \citep{RS}), we do not completely supplant the Max-3SAT-based results from \citep{RS}. Faced with a problem whose exact solution cannot be computed in polynomial time, if we refuse to compromise on compute time, there are several ways in which we might make the problem ``easier." The first way is by deciding to accept an answer of approximate quality, and another way is to only require a method to perform well for a restricted class of inputs.
%Rolnick and Spencer showed that NP-Hardness of the $(\frac{3}{4}+\epsilon)$-Fractional Gr\"{o}bner Basis Problem holds even for an extremely restricted class of inputs. They achieve this restriction by leveraging a refinement of H{\aa}stad's Max 3-SAT hardness result due to Guraswami and Khot for a special case of Max 3-SAT known as ``Max No-Mixed Exactly 3-SAT." Under their reduction this model yields a particularly-simple polynomial system. For example, in \citep{RS} the variety associated with each polynomial is symmetric in the variables that appear in the polynomial, which is not a property of the polynomial systems in our reductions below. Further, while in \cite{RS} the main proof can be adapted to give a robust hardness result that has no structural constraint on the ignored set of generators, our proofs for the $q$-Fractional model have no obvious analogy like this. In particular, our reductions construct polynomial systems that lack the symmetry that Rolnick and Spencer exploit in their Max-3SAT-based arguments.

