{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 512, "variables": 32, "coeff_bound": 1000000, "mix_bound": 19}, "seed": 913066543, "solved": "solved", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": true, "verify_reason": "ok", "reply": "To find the reduced lexicographic Groebner basis, we need to find the constants $a_i$ such that $x_i^2 - a_i = 0$ for $i=1, \\dots, 32$.\nLet $S = \\sum_{j=1}^{32} a_j$. Since $Q_2 = \\sum_{j=1}^{32} x_j^2$, each polynomial in the basis is evaluated as $u_i S + \\lambda_i a_{p_i} = c_i$. This gives a series of independent linear equations for $S$ and $a_{p_i}$ for $i=1, \\dots, 32$.\n\nBy reducing the given equations for each $p$, we can isolate $a_p$ in terms of $S$ and establish modular constraints on $S$ because each $a_p$ must be an integer:\n- For $p=1$, $13 S + 7 a_1 = 199651775 \\implies S \\equiv 6 \\pmod 7$\n- For $p=11$, $10 S + 9 a_{11} = 152016897 \\implies S \\equiv 3 \\pmod 9$\n- For $p=16$, $7 S + 8 a_{16} = 112441854 \\implies S \\equiv 2 \\pmod 8$\n- For $p=26$, $6 S + 5 a_{26} = 90103437 \\implies S \\equiv 2 \\pmod 5$\n- For $p=17$, $7 S + 13 a_{17} = 108017798 \\implies S \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}$\n- For $p=21$, $16 S + 17 a_{21} = 242231739 \\implies S \\equiv 3 \\pmod{17}$\n- For $p=8$, $6 S + 11 a_8 = 93613656 \\implies S \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}$\n\nUsing the Chinese Remainder Theorem on these pairwise coprime moduli (with product $7 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 5 \\times 13 \\times 17 \\times 11 = 6126120$), we find:\n$S \\equiv 2739162 \\pmod{6126120}$\n\nThe problem strictly bounds $a_p \\in [1, 1000000]$. We can restrict $S$ by using these bounds across our localized equations (e.g., $6 S + a_{19} = 90930666 \\implies S < 15155111$ and $4 S + 7 a_{22} = 60019718 \\implies S \\le 15004927$).\nFiltering our candidate values of $S = 2739162 + 6126120k$ using these constraints, for $k=2$, we land exactly at $S = 14991402$, which satisfies all inequality bounds simultaneously.\n\nWe substitute $S = 14991402$ back into our equations to determine all 32 values of $a_p$:\n- $a_1 = (199651775 - 13 \\times 14991402) / 7 = 680507$\n- $a_2 = 30713957 - 2 \\times 14991402 = 731153$\n- $a_3 = 120586406 - 8 \\times 14991402 = 655190$\n- $a_4 = (126137017 - 8 \\times 14991402) / 7 = 886543$\n- $a_5 = (196271608 - 13 \\times 14991402) / 14 = 98813$\n- $a_6 = (139987384 - 9 \\times 14991402) / 14 = 361769$\n- $a_7 = (31005354 - 2 \\times 14991402) / 3 = 340850$\n- $a_8 = (93613656 - 6 \\times 14991402) / 11 = 333204$\n- $a_9 = (65671364 - 4 \\times 14991402) / 7 = 815108$\n- $a_{10} = (16448302 - 14991402) / 2 = 728450$\n- $a_{11} = (152016897 - 10 \\times 14991402) / 9 = 233653$\n- $a_{12} = (15800992 - 14991402) / 2 = 404795$\n- $a_{13} = (147219296 - 9 \\times 14991402) / 17 = 723334$\n- $a_{14} = (82702329 - 5 \\times 14991402) / 17 = 455607$\n- $a_{15} = (113583225 - 7 \\times 14991402) / 9 = 960379$\n- $a_{16} = (112441854 - 7 \\times 14991402) / 8 = 937755$\n- $a_{17} = (108017798 - 7 \\times 14991402) / 13 = 236768$\n- $a_{18} = (45669450 - 3 \\times 14991402) / 2 = 347622$\n- $a_{19} = 90930666 - 6 \\times 14991402 = 982254$\n- $a_{20} = 165367776 - 11 \\times 14991402 = 462354$\n- $a_{21} = (242231739 - 16 \\times 14991402) / 17 = 139371$\n- $a_{22} = (60019718 - 4 \\times 14991402) / 7 = 7730$\n- $a_{23} = (45199018 - 3 \\times 14991402) / 2 = 112406$\n- $a_{24} = (245439227 - 16 \\times 14991402) / 7 = 796685$\n- $a_{25} = (61476288 - 4 \\times 14991402) / 3 = 503560$\n- $a_{26} = (90103437 - 6 \\times 14991402) / 5 = 31005$\n- $a_{27} = (92655557 - 6 \\times 14991402) / 5 = 541429$\n- $a_{28} = (197852026 - 13 \\times 14991402) / 4 = 740950$\n- $a_{29} = 30063885 - 2 \\times 14991402 = 81081$\n- $a_{30} = (31158636 - 2 \\times 14991402) / 3 = 391944$\n- $a_{31} = (195408219 - 13 \\times 14991402) / 3 = 173331$\n- $a_{32} = (15853620 - 14991402) / 9 = 95802$\n\n<answer>[680507,731153,655190,886543,98813,361769,340850,333204,815108,728450,233653,40